## Problem 1: Two Sum

### Explanation

Given an array of integers `nums` and an integer `target`, return indices of the two numbers such that they add up to `target`.

You may assume that each input would have exactly one solution, and you may not use the same element twice.

### Code Snippet

```java
public class TwoSum {
    public static int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        int[] result = new int[2];
        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int complement = target - nums[i];
            if (map.containsKey(complement)) {
                result[0] = map.get(complement);
                result[1] = i;
                return result;
            }
            map.put(nums[i], i);
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {2, 7, 11, 15};
        int target = 9;
        int[] result = twoSum(nums, target);
        System.out.println("Indices: " + result[0] + ", " + result[1]);
    }
}
```

### Explanation

The `twoSum` method takes an array of integers `nums` and an integer `target` as input. It returns an array of two integers representing the indices of the two numbers that add up to `target`.

The method uses a `HashMap` to store the elements of the array and their corresponding indices. It iterates through the array and calculates the complement of each element with respect to the `target`. If the complement is found in the `HashMap`, it means that the two numbers have been found, and their indices are returned. Otherwise, the current element and its index are added to the `HashMap`.

In the `main` method, an example array `nums` and a `target` value are provided. The `twoSum` method is called with these inputs, and the resulting indices are printed.

### Time Complexity

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the length of the input array `nums`. This is because the method iterates through the array once, and the `HashMap` operations (put and get) have an average time complexity of O(1).

### Space Complexity

The space complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the length of the input array `nums`. This is because the `HashMap` stores at most n elements, where each element has a key-value pair.

